    "case_id": "416",
    "case_summary": "A woman in her 80s was referred for a cardiac evaluation after results from an electrocardiogram performed during a routine health evaluation showed a wide QRS complex. The patient had no previous medical records with her at the evaluation. The patient denied any history of chest pain.\n\nPhysical examination revealed the apex of the heart located at the right side of the chest accompanied by hepatic dullness that was located in the left subcostal region.\n\n- ECG Findings:    Initial ECG showed:    - Wide complex rhythm at rate of 76 beats/min with right bundle branch block morphology    - QRS duration of 120 milliseconds    - Negative P waves with pathological Q waves in leads I and aVL    - Positive P waves in lead V1    - Biphasic P waves in leads V2 through V6    - Pathological Q waves in leads V5 and V6        Corrected ECG (after electrode relocation):    - Demonstrated normal sinus rhythm with complete right bundle branch block  - Imaging:    Title: Transthoracic Echocardiogram    Description: Showed right atrium and right ventricle laid on the left side and the left atrium and left ventricle on the right side with its apex directed to the right",
